abstract:
A gamma-ray burst fireball is likely to contain an admixture of neutrons. Inelastic collisions between differentially streaming protons and neutrons in the fireball produce ${\ensuremath{
u}}_{\ensuremath{\mu}}$ $({\overline{\ensuremath{
u}}}_{\ensuremath{\mu}})$ of $\ensuremath{\sim}10\mathrm{GeV}$ as well as ${\ensuremath{
u}}_{e}$ $({\overline{\ensuremath{
u}}}_{e})$ of $\ensuremath{\sim}5\mathrm{GeV}$, which could produce $\ensuremath{\sim}7\mathrm{events}/\mathrm{year}$ in ${\mathrm{km}}^{3}$ detectors, if the neutron abundance is comparable to that of protons. Photons of $\ensuremath{\sim}10\mathrm{GeV}$ from ${\ensuremath{\pi}}^{0}$ decay and $\ensuremath{\sim}100\mathrm{MeV}$ ${\overline{\ensuremath{
u}}}_{e}$ from neutron decay are also produced, but will be difficult to detect. Photons with energies $\ensuremath{\lesssim}1\mathrm{MeV}$ from shocks following neutron decay produce a characteristic signal which may be distinguishable from the proton-related MeV photons.
